" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540036}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Containing a \"Greater Than\" Symbol"} :step/id #uuid "ad4c4a48-5234-5c54-86de-eb13ccd09abb" :step/math-academy-id 221735}] :activity/title "The CDF of the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167037}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503807} :activity/id #uuid "a0a97922-c29e-5eb9-a00b-a08031d2cade" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049899 :topic/id #uuid "1cb109e6-3987-5c41-9a55-81f03d70697e" :topic/math-academy-id 3274} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "380c1b05-6c1c-5ffd-bc5c-a04c05c5c687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503080} {:answer/id #uuid "5577f8d1-9f94-5c6f-ba9c-92f1736f818d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4558" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503144} {:answer/id #uuid "6e80960c-3886-58dd-b64a-4e2557138902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503203} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d579dc-32ed-5798-98c2-1ace09760f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503400} {:answer/id #uuid "d834d903-ffee-58a0-92db-9ab0051f1e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5470" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503412} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20cf601-9154-52ab-ba75-6f001245e333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b92762e3-576d-55cb-b009-a97e0be6338a" :question/math-academy-id "q-225067" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(4)$ given below, compute $P(3\\le X<6).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0916\\,$ | $\\,0.2381\\,$ | $\\,0.4335\\,$ | $\\,0.6288\\,$ | $\\,0.7851\\,$ | $\\,0.8893\\,$ | $\\,0.9489\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X<6) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(3\\le X<6) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(3\\le X<6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<6) & =F(5)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.7851-0.2381 \\\\  & =0.5470.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "21d06828-9a3e-5396-9154-89624675cb97" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79a1b4b4-0c8f-509b-96be-e81e96d0cf38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503229} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bb07aaf-fd25-5aa9-a148-43a824ecca08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3df054eb-2d42-57cd-ac48-5335659c80b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-225055" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(5)$ given below, compute $P(2\\le X\\le 4).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0067\\,$ | $\\,0.0404\\,$ | $\\,0.1247\\,$ | $\\,0.2650\\,$ | $\\,0.4405\\,$ | $\\,0.6160\\,$ | $\\,0.7622\\,$ |\n\n$P(2\\le X\\le 4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\})+P(X\\in \\{2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4) \\\\  & =F(1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 4) & =F(4)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.4405-0.0404 \\\\  & =0.4001.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84e1ee1a-4b89-5aa4-b30a-3a5d79ef978f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072af78c-0cf5-586f-bc53-ad8197623ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3239" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502995} {:answer/id #uuid "23093e91-9f8a-526f-b9fe-a4c366c326dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503048} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c5ad47-e307-560d-aa30-f635fcf6ddd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503318} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc0b37a-e911-5ce6-b543-981451964978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5307" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503430} {:answer/id #uuid "fe79ce86-a670-57ab-ab9f-302046099afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4557" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502995} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d92468-cb05-508e-bcd0-42d2b482d3c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "951aecd2-9a03-5921-a647-8badf487101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-224995" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(9)$ given below, find the value of $P(X\\le 7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0550\\,$ | $\\,0.1157\\,$ | $\\,0.2068\\,$ | $\\,0.3239\\,$ | $\\,0.4557\\,$ | $\\,0.5874\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =F(7) \\\\  & =0.3239.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8fc62da4-3a4c-5a53-bfc2-793fee1d81cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "871b12f6-b300-5aad-9c37-0f6b3ba75025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7664" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503257} {:answer/id #uuid "b364a7c4-21e7-540e-8e24-cda36eac8906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503347} {:answer/id #uuid "bf85c091-12aa-55ce-9c65-94896b0eada1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7166" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503363} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a0a61b-0e09-5891-9fdf-a1c86f0ad38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503448} {:answer/id #uuid "f31f3176-4cec-53f7-9c8b-c339b62c86e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8159" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503347} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b47d28-52ea-5db5-8d68-c29eb95437d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c63f903-029e-5df7-9333-d7481c32658b" :question/math-academy-id "q-224997" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(X<9).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ | $\\,0.7166\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,8\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 8) \\\\  & =F(8) \\\\  & =0.5925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b2ab6c64-abb3-5af6-ae48-fa7ca76e80c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d77a7660-717e-5f04-b7b5-229f67d8428a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2936" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503408} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dda7cdb-4dec-5905-b4f9-bd936abe49ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f03e36d-2709-59cb-981a-892881905e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-225087" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(3.6)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.5152\\,$ | $\\,0.7064\\,$ | $\\,0.8441\\,$ | $\\,0.9267\\,$ | $\\,0.9692\\,$ | $\\,0.9883\\,$ | $\\,0.9960\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\ge 5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=P(X>4).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-F(4) \\\\  & =1-0.7064 \\\\  & =0.2936.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cea9281b-2c6b-5557-b332-58513c78c6dd"}] :activity/title "The CDF of the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502972}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540167} :activity/id #uuid "fada443e-8034-5b67-8dc8-1b9292803e76" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049899 :topic/id #uuid "1cb109e6-3987-5c41-9a55-81f03d70697e" :topic/math-academy-id 3274} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc688eb-61f5-52d8-bf95-9b58a000fa0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539537} :answer-field/id #uuid "170b17fe-2985-5e8e-adef-2c16c5db1659" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8455b7b-7c5b-5bab-ac5b-3afa70d37c46" :question/math-academy-id "q-224966" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(4<X\\le 6).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0138\\,$ | $\\,0.0424\\,$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ |\n\n$P(4<X\\le 6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(6) & =P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\})+P(X\\in \\{5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 4)+P(4<X\\le 6) \\\\  & =F(4)+P(4<X\\le 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 6) & =F(6)-F(4) \\\\  & =0.3134-0.0996 \\\\  & =0.2138.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15fb34ea-7c36-5de6-89f0-352505c9199d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540181}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad3d3640-8bab-5540-a659-263ab3ab21ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163963} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1db4a4-1cae-534d-ad7a-abff4b20a72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163964} {:answer/id #uuid "24b70d04-9391-5587-a416-3485e5b2bd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6063" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163965} {:answer/id #uuid "2a914e27-67f8-571c-b02c-1214d09a85c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4457" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163966} {:answer/id #uuid "3d63a520-54a9-5f47-8432-5d7d336601b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3837" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163967} :answer-field/id #uuid "cedfd9ab-59be-578b-9247-c2238adee679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74407edc-ca03-5605-ae34-cdfeb4fc24d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-224486" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(6)$ given below, compute $P(2<X\\le 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0620\\,$ | $\\,0.1512\\,$ | $\\,0.2851\\,$ | $\\,0.4457\\,$ | $\\,0.6063\\,$ | $\\,0.7440\\,$ | $\\,0.8472\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(2<X\\le 5) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(2<X\\le 5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5) & =F(5)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.4457-0.0620 \\\\  & =0.3837.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "296bb2e2-9a40-524a-8c06-52de5f6f888d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "888157ba-107f-5a5c-97c9-d6b5387c773f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6761" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539707} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a0816c-2f64-5562-b7f4-1cfc7a5ea04a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27647abc-efb3-57c0-813d-ce357897815d" :question/math-academy-id "q-225086" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(9)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 8).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0550\\,$ | $\\,0.1157\\,$ | $\\,0.2068\\,$ | $\\,0.3239\\,$ | $\\,0.4557\\,$ | $\\,0.5874\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\ge 8)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 8)=P(X>7).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 8) & =P(X>7) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 7) \\\\  & =1-F(7) \\\\  & =1-0.3239 \\\\  & =0.6761.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "492933d6-6fda-58ce-927c-d810684d1abf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e38d1e74-616c-5faf-bb57-5aca03f53456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5793" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163957} {:answer/id #uuid "0d98539a-d569-5654-b7f9-1929c49b24a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163958} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3c8097-94e9-5fa4-b808-35465cd058b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163959} {:answer/id #uuid "261dfc0b-c182-55d2-a867-3c1ec5a93ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163960} {:answer/id #uuid "569b296c-fbd5-5a62-a832-1e70faf97df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163961} :answer-field/id #uuid "41432890-ee26-58ff-bd92-b48d392c79d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69667702-28d4-55ff-ad76-b5f0a166efd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-224481" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(11)$ given below, compute $P(X<10).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.1432\\,$ | $\\,0.2320\\,$ | $\\,0.3405\\,$ | $\\,0.4599\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.6887\\,$ | $\\,0.7813\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<10) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,9\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 9) \\\\  & =F(9) \\\\  & =0.3405.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c2457db8-1ad7-55ba-80b6-98094e23625b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fae328c-89f2-563a-a5a4-668ae4227a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539747} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2ff7417-ca9e-560f-ae90-1cd48c8905de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17398103-0726-57d3-aad8-24938a444c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-224994" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(7)$ given below, find the value of $P(X\\le 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0009\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ | $\\,0.0296\\,$ | $\\,0.0818\\,$ | $\\,0.1730\\,$ | $\\,0.3007\\,$ | $\\,0.4497\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\le 5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =F(5) \\\\  & =0.3007.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fff841e9-2a22-5953-a62f-baf22457d682" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540135}}] :activity/title "The CDF of the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539477}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167962} :activity/id #uuid "f98be8d2-8501-53a4-b5df-d4f1ac4126c5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050260 :topic/id #uuid "3a43c523-581b-58c8-a48d-f665d05c6e1f" :topic/math-academy-id 3218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168050 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x \\in A$ and $0$ if $x \\notin A.$ \n![](images/c1/c12d2f59eb7eb9730fc7576bb426cff90a3cff4ac8577c3f212db8101197300b.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we introduce a more general notation that allows us to write indicator functions for any condition, not just set membership.\n\nThe **Iverson bracket notation** $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ takes a predicate $P$ and returns\n![](images/4d/4dbe29e205fecf97bd7d4c0bfbf810c7336a6a50198fa35319e7ae8b0ac505c0.svg)\n\nFor example:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[3 > 2] = 1$ because $3 > 2$ is true.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[5 \\leq 4] = 0$ because $5 \\leq 4$ is false.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[7 \\in \\{2, 5, 7\\}] = 1$ because $7$ is an element of $\\{2, 5, 7\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "83dcb8a1-56ef-59d0-addc-dfaa18628695" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14816 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "691d9e29-1625-5c4d-8958-88262d0bc24b" :step/math-academy-id 610829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166577 :question/id #uuid "5ebf00aa-406d-5286-b4ae-0f0044ce9b4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144f097c-0080-5857-b992-8996f194ca22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049002}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521540}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions to Check Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "9565a3bd-15ac-5aa0-88fe-2e6938ac1515" :step/math-academy-id 610830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168059 :tutorial/content "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A].\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A.$ \n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n![](images/cd/cd01819adb510c2b439f44089b1bd3cbd1f49e671779039fdf2f6be62a39810f.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a719d20-4e48-50a9-9e39-db7296bd47d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14942 :tutorial/title "Set Notation and Bracket Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c85ef936-9305-53f6-8db5-6415695e3c58" :step/math-academy-id 610833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166579 :question/id #uuid "c831e4d2-736d-5ee1-9c0e-55cee535f361" :question/math-academy-id "e-22020"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e434dbd-b33c-5997-b3ba-043233cfbc89" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554632}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Indicator Notations"} :step/id #uuid "3c0f9089-52c4-5d6c-bd45-8b5935705854" :step/math-academy-id 610834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168051 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0,$ the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i.$ If $x_i \\leq 0,$ the indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values $x_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2.$ Using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1[5>0]\\cdot 5+1[-3>0]\\cdot (-3)+1[2>0]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5+0+2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]$ counts the positive values. For this data, the count is $1 + 0 + 1 = 2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc59a9ec-2473-5942-9ab7-f81210d7426c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14817 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Sums"} :step/id #uuid "68e458ec-f61c-5b8a-ad94-e1c6511e3aeb" :step/math-academy-id 610837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166969 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166578 :question/id #uuid "1687d5ff-6b48-52d9-a947-3425227af366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1900018f-1bac-5623-b45f-e3d9c89f5d74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554636}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "559e173c-cc9a-5be6-9a22-191e4f99a000" :step/math-academy-id 610838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168060 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n![](images/33/336dc8080661db77e7ac701204aa6a32075bc7f4bd06625f4e8467e87a5c075c.svg)\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n|x| = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nSince the conditions $x \\geq 0$ and $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0.$ This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-4| & =1[-4\\ge 0]\\cdot (-4)+1[-4<0]\\cdot (-(-4)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50ebf85-232e-5ea7-aed0-188bbb2467bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14963 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "ec8855d0-be42-5c66-a902-f6d870071621" :step/math-academy-id 611696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167967}}] :activity/title "Indicator Functions for Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167001}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554661} :activity/id #uuid "465539fa-aa06-5c88-a039-a2b61aa93569" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050260 :topic/id #uuid "3a43c523-581b-58c8-a48d-f665d05c6e1f" :topic/math-academy-id 3218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd697bf3-bab3-5be7-93b9-af592dc078ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554352} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb32b44-fa3a-53b5-9c67-b4cbe35e057e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3d61585-203e-5ae2-ba7d-32b154b3cecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-347438" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=-6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=7\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1$ equals $2{x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $1$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1\n$$\n\nadds $2{x}_{i}$ for nonnegative values and $1$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-6\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[-1\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[4\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[7\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1 & =1+1+2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =1+1+0+8+14 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0e4446b3-5739-5e1b-a31f-10c169910b46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554663}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156a71be-c9a4-5ae7-9a2c-02e2f98c3a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553911} :answer-field/id #uuid "034b7137-7dce-507f-82b0-3f725c6f8c4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f030361e-8a29-5e63-bca8-4e28e1ba5da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-346677" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=9,\\,{x}_{2}=-8,\\,{x}_{3}=-1,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[9>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[-8>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[4>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9+0+0+4+2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2555c50a-befa-5681-b2d0-0c5336c8f67e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a42e783-06ac-540f-a993-5fee32a5d1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[-1=C]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554115} {:answer/id #uuid "905baf65-bc02-52ab-a96c-06c275629272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[-1\\notin \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554181} {:answer/id #uuid "c64a4829-6ad8-5110-9db6-5fd9a585d15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[C\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554304} {:answer/id #uuid "c77659db-76b7-535a-9c58-254c1f6de06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554307} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d06cfb-9a89-5639-8808-7411f0dd598c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}\\in -1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554307} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c55f3e7-608f-546e-9238-bec098222d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8cf80bf-23f2-50e5-8be6-fd421cafc0d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346662" :question/problem "Let $C=\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{C}(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{C}(x)$ of a set $C$ equals $1$ if $x\\in C$ and $0$ if $x\\notin C.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(x)=1[x\\in C].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(-1)=1[-1\\in C]=1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3398d19e-2a4e-5ffe-8497-c4c076f1a2b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9e8e21-b550-5dab-acbe-bf94504b1d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{\\{2,4,6,8\\}}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553901} {:answer/id #uuid "23fcb709-e1df-5686-a023-45de9c7332ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{5}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553932} {:answer/id #uuid "6acf5d37-f233-58a7-b644-4950c5f08b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{5}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554078} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec50b2b-0fb1-535c-bee6-07f2da381e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{B}(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554088} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7b8b0c-b17c-5fc2-aad1-cf9ecfd4c52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{B}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554420} :answer-field/id #uuid "68354444-5465-5b9a-9ef0-8c6ea884ef5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6df0b1a8-9045-561a-b52c-2dfb375e5b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-349167" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[5\\in B]?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[5\\in B]$ has $x=5$ and $A=B.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[5\\in B]={1}_{B}(5).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a5fb79e-7cd3-5538-834f-01836d139a39"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d52dde-78fc-56d2-a22f-16743942f394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[\\{0,5,10\\}\\in 7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553963} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5a4ce9-38a3-50c2-ab37-2b31fa255565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[7=D]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553982} {:answer/id #uuid "3f98e784-15fe-5da6-bf43-5d7bde990e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[D\\in \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553984} {:answer/id #uuid "929abffe-450c-5006-a4e0-9188f9b6910d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[7\\in \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554193} {:answer/id #uuid "caeec9e3-866d-5050-ab7c-4b6736d7497b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[7\\notin \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554193} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd8b1eb9-905f-5eaa-b94d-45d4a5b08f18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7ecead24-f472-509d-9b09-182eae6554e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346663" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{0,5,10\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{D}(7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{D}(x)$ of a set $D$ equals $1$ if $x\\in D$ and $0$ if $x\\notin D.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(x)=1[x\\in D].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(7)=1[7\\in D]=1[7\\in \\{0,5,10\\}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bfd99f70-2dec-5500-aa3b-df54e1e74a81" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554656}}] :activity/title "Indicator Functions for Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553844}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106620} :activity/id #uuid "f530f18b-11b2-5bde-8332-6af2fe3c20eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356 :topic/id #uuid "421dabda-4549-5ad6-b909-e9e0b37e1c41" :topic/math-academy-id 1286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107074 :tutorial/content "The **scalar triple product** of three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is defined as $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFor example, suppose we have three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 1,1,-2 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle 0,3,1 \\rangle.$ Let's compute their scalar triple product, $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFirst, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =7i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨7,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The scalar triple product takes its name from the fact that it multiplies three vectors together and always results in a scalar.\n\nLater, we will learn some geometric intuition behind the scalar triple product. But for now, we will focus on computing the scalar triple product." :tutorial/id #uuid "28eff4c1-2c88-5799-b15d-0ab9f27be09b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54962f85-eb4f-5689-8e98-692b298d15d1" :step/math-academy-id 15857 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593 :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552778} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552776}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "3a87ffcc-ec7d-55bd-9fb9-bf8d091686aa" :step/math-academy-id 15858 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107075 :tutorial/content "It turns out that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nThat is to say, if $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's focus on computing the scalar triple product using the determinant above." :tutorial/id #uuid "67915540-ba40-5a4a-a7b0-6d81497d64c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2953 :tutorial/title "The Definition of the Scalar Triple Product Via the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "49837287-74a9-55df-aeef-eb0b8fd6f361" :step/math-academy-id 15859 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594 :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419787}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "8fd2fcb8-9be2-5a30-a126-347cf62d3bd6" :step/math-academy-id 15860 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107076 :tutorial/content "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n1\\. If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n  ![](images/82/827b78de142e085b00c9c7eccb43ba32b30861ef45d91b53b1f35c86a67b78f4.svg)\n\n2\\. If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n  ![](images/ac/acadadda23e53448493a31441e2d3e53e89b6a320d6b4040c7567a2489d3cff3.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "41cd1550-dd91-5029-a397-fa1417d1e160" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2954 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Scalar Triple Product"} :step/id #uuid "29a88567-7fa3-590d-9466-ca0095d38194" :step/math-academy-id 15861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105595 :question/id #uuid "483b758a-9b14-5a72-87e5-70f27a0acf48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4349"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535224} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535204}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"} :step/id #uuid "06a0d838-fa82-5fdb-bf4d-ea928e28bcaa" :step/math-academy-id 15862 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107077 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\cdot (|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|-{a}_{2}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|+{a}_{3}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99f752d5-13c8-5b3e-aeee-383770b0db33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2955 :tutorial/title "A Proof of the Formula for the Scalar Triple Product"} :step/id #uuid "4b903237-363d-5bc1-826b-fb982e1c9d41" :step/math-academy-id 15863}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105963}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408114} :activity/id #uuid "541e0bf9-d38c-5752-a23f-c4c9f0e075e8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356 :topic/id #uuid "421dabda-4549-5ad6-b909-e9e0b37e1c41" :topic/math-academy-id 1286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9f5fa0-b5b6-5984-816e-05ebf16c604e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261385} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e506eb-0c73-54cd-bea5-69edf125c21e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261741} {:answer/id #uuid "daff916a-8d3a-5192-a81e-8d54ac5e6b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261811} {:answer/id #uuid "f263a5a4-8c23-5633-8f3f-d1dee00dda4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261994} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a8659d-0b1d-58eb-a190-a4dc181002b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262004} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d44782d-0883-5e02-9033-b08b78209c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6324ef53-4779-5e72-aa0d-0e3d70a28579" :question/math-academy-id "q-128356" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a),$ if $b\\times c=⟨6,-1,3⟩$ and $a=⟨3,5,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨3,5,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6+5\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18-5-12 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "09e1afca-7cd5-5ac9-8a1c-381614403a24" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ef0874-008a-597f-b2cb-02be44cd3944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408103} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8dcecd-d942-5c7a-b18e-cb5a2602acc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408104} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf98593-9506-526d-9be6-5176a031c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408105} {:answer/id #uuid "a019b149-6685-5c94-9ead-07affcb89def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408106} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d39875-cd66-5fd6-9485-32650cce5157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408103} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd432bd-eb14-5f9d-bc51-e98dfa9d5d17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32b7570a-855d-5799-88e4-c24fe54119de" :question/math-academy-id "q-20053" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,2,-3⟩$, $b=⟨2,-1,2⟩$ and $c=⟨-2,1,1⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)-2(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2)-3(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6-12+0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9d367acc-118a-5937-a41c-6d7159bd7657" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bb8d9-a3bb-5f41-8ede-1ac64d1b2be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285204} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6f5fc5-825c-54cb-8f8c-ee058c85459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286495} {:answer/id #uuid "eef31947-0a99-57ff-8618-686a5c2e30a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286980} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a78fa7-b3e6-5170-9dc3-909924cfd396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287018} {:answer/id #uuid "f992ea26-69d2-5514-84ea-1987e40856cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286495} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd48eaf-9d9e-5aaf-ad75-c7d452ae0613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0db80f94-e2a5-583e-875d-ce7dd7157cf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20062" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a)$, if $b\\times c=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $a=⟨1,-2,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf136a46-14cf-58e5-ae21-1b4fd4c26349" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a8d4cf-2a52-5eb3-932c-de54f969d498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339176} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2029a1-5daa-5d17-915b-5cb3db9d0a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339665} {:answer/id #uuid "7364ef2c-a924-50d5-ae40-c9a6106f8bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340152} {:answer/id #uuid "84d38775-5cfe-5497-a2ea-945dc01057b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f005ac-dfb9-5a8e-9f29-140e8e17a482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} :answer-field/id #uuid "50438105-46e0-54c5-8329-966f684eb5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fc420fe9-802f-5344-9de7-9b53d8435b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-73462" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,1,2⟩$, $q=⟨1,-2,1⟩$ and $r=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)-2(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-20+1-14 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c9595afb-61e2-5d8f-929b-2f523416e52a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28f1b85-eac0-597c-848e-a2bdbc20cee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408107} :answer-field/id #uuid "9284f3f7-2649-559e-ade1-b845ad584b38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "627a3422-ad6a-5d23-b76b-6cf6cb390db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-317318" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-1,2,4⟩$, $b=⟨0,3,1⟩$ and $c=⟨2,5,0⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n\n$-15$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 5)\\,-\\,2\\,(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,4\\,(0\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(-5)\\,-\\,2\\,(-2)\\,+\\,4\\,(-6) \\\\  & =5+4-24 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca58aca0-69d3-55b1-a46c-485edbd29743"}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408102}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419792} :activity/id #uuid "e3670cc4-3e0e-52dd-b203-84b0f2da8c90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356 :topic/id #uuid "421dabda-4549-5ad6-b909-e9e0b37e1c41" :topic/math-academy-id 1286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a8d4cf-2a52-5eb3-932c-de54f969d498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339176} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2029a1-5daa-5d17-915b-5cb3db9d0a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339665} {:answer/id #uuid "7364ef2c-a924-50d5-ae40-c9a6106f8bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340152} {:answer/id #uuid "84d38775-5cfe-5497-a2ea-945dc01057b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f005ac-dfb9-5a8e-9f29-140e8e17a482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} :answer-field/id #uuid "50438105-46e0-54c5-8329-966f684eb5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fc420fe9-802f-5344-9de7-9b53d8435b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-73462" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,1,2⟩$, $q=⟨1,-2,1⟩$ and $r=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)-2(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-20+1-14 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2145564f-b143-5694-829e-a9e30f5dc092" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419790}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069decae-ea7d-53a6-9d7f-6cd1604d5e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419779} :answer-field/id #uuid "0082553f-6022-5a48-bc6b-791b1c483f07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "944d3b7e-a470-59f8-92c4-a8abf2a8b72b" :question/math-academy-id "q-317329" :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a)$, if $-5a\\cdot ((6b)\\times (4c))=120.$\n\n$b\\cdot (c\\times a)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a\\cdot ((6b)\\times (4c)) & =120 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =120 \\\\ (-120)[a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =120 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "69f520c8-2f68-5cc8-8bb4-1d0fcdb03028"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aadbe221-860c-5e54-a0de-bd7fb0bef36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea71914-4f17-5f64-8016-140e481b698d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20209d26-6af3-5176-8409-17df2aefc0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-317313" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,-3,0⟩$, $b=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $c=⟨2,0,-4⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(2\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 0)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,((-2)\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,0\\,((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1\\,(-8)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,(8)\\,+\\,0\\,(-4) \\\\  & =-8+24+0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ce27cb2-7851-562f-a805-9613dfe990e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f56b9f8-00ca-5814-abb9-6c5a7caba35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419780} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd14090-bb12-5afc-8250-d9460b7c0f27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f15d30b5-fe4a-5207-9935-fd7503715554" :question/math-academy-id "q-317326" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,0,-1⟩$, $b=⟨-3,-1,4⟩$ and $c=⟨5,-2,-2⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\,((-1)(-2)-4\\cdot (-2))\\,-\\,0\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 5)\\,+\\,(-1)\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =(-2)\\,(10)\\,-\\,0\\,(-14)\\,+\\,(-1)\\,(11) \\\\  & =-20+0-11 \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "85680ae8-e720-54c1-a502-614781ce6410" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343abe13-b014-58c0-913d-2fc9f7a11d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285270} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3b1e45-a9c8-5b28-b558-ec4bc793eb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285496} {:answer/id #uuid "a878dcad-4ddf-585b-a29e-6f1bfa1aa273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286324} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e6bcc3-f1d7-59c6-953b-c03961aeaf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286630} {:answer/id #uuid "fbedc400-7438-5567-8dfa-d1cfcb39be79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287087} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a770db-2d9f-5df9-858e-0db4cb77fe94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae812812-92a4-5aa7-85f9-50adcd0043cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-20059" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b)$, if $3a\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}b\\times 3c)=18\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}b\\times 3c) & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $c$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (c\\times b) & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $c$ and $a$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d94231c6-3192-5dc5-bd63-54e41758bbbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419788}}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419778}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106573} :activity/id #uuid "0a27c7be-9281-5ee1-a624-d9ff46333578" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438 :topic/id #uuid "493479ab-e6ae-52f5-9c1a-827aa0443283" :topic/math-academy-id 1359} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107065 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ below. How can we determine the intervals where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative?\n\nThe first step is to identify the points where the derivative is zero. Here, the critical points are $x=2$ (a local maximum) and $x=4$ (a local minimum), and $f'(x) = 0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/de/de01a8dec747f86d6b276431ef02675d3827028508be9aa9ca3699ca4e6bcd6c.png)\n\nThe next step is to identify where the function is increasing and decreasing. Below, the intervals where $f(x)$ is increasing are shown in blue, and the intervals where $f(x)$ is decreasing are shown in green.\n\n![](images/10/10edc10cefa3cc48713795e0e2f40b05ba6174e2da7aa5698a02fc35444b1c45.png)\n\nSince we know where the function is increasing or decreasing, we can determine where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative.\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are positive, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)>0.\n$$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are negative, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)<0.\n$$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be  summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,2) & 2 & (2,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{'} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, \n\n- $f'(x)>0$ on $(-\\infty,2) \\cup (4,+\\infty),$ while\n\n- $f'(x)<0$ on $(2,4).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a75bc2f2-f4fa-50c8-9eaa-230212f35132" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2661 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "136ca6f2-4f48-519a-8e2b-fcd52eaff347" :step/math-academy-id 12776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105566 :question/id #uuid "6b6ba1b8-5041-57c3-bcbb-239af0faa4dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341972}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"} :step/id #uuid "fe83e38b-e03f-5af9-907e-72d9f01b87e5" :step/math-academy-id 12783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107067 :tutorial/content "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing, we can calculate the derivative and then solve the appropriate inequality.\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9bdb8755-6e15-5d0b-b4da-b1a720b6dc4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2665 :tutorial/title "Calculating Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing or Decreasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "bff9aeb3-19b2-547d-b422-3a3680105413" :step/math-academy-id 12785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105568 :question/id #uuid "7be416a1-e729-58a4-bc3c-d622d058eba8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050617}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400667}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "10ad6cd4-75ee-5a9e-9c37-f75681712cd6" :step/math-academy-id 12790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105571 :question/id #uuid "cc1f0cb5-5cca-5a70-a89f-2983a64e273f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3731"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402991}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "371b12ac-a46c-5fdd-8712-331bed644307" :step/math-academy-id 12793 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105572 :question/id #uuid "88cdcba5-2572-5a6a-aa99-826b80cb1d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-3734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341982}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"} :step/id #uuid "27be8546-1f29-51fe-90dd-a240e31ced9f" :step/math-academy-id 12796}] :activity/title "Determining Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing or Decreasing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105992}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825} :activity/id #uuid "ccbd1570-797c-5564-8927-e502aa05caec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527 :topic/id #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" :topic/math-academy-id 3029} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163179 :tutorial/content "We can do the same trick using the **elementary column operations** instead of row operations.\n\nThere are three types of elementary *column* operations, which are entirely analogous to the corresponding row operations:\n\n- Switching any two columns: \n\n$$\nC_i \\leftrightarrow C_j\n$$\n\n- Multiplying (each element of) a column by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nC_i := k C_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column: \n\n$$\nC_j := C_j+k C_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** \n\n- Unlike elementary row operations, elementary *column* operations change the solutions of a system of linear equations. This is because column operations alter the coefficients associated with each variable across the system, changing the equations.\n\n- The exception is swapping two columns - this doesn't alter the solutions *provided that* we swap the corresponding variables in the solution vector. For example, if we swap the first and second columns, we must treat the second variable as the first and vice versa. The solution set remains unchanged because the relationships between the variables and their coefficients are preserved." :tutorial/id #uuid "e947134c-e909-5a3b-85a6-d284e04816e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10652 :tutorial/title "Column Operations"} :step/id #uuid "ce002306-ea12-5d28-8c26-a1710d2f4327" :step/math-academy-id 218771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163214 :tutorial/content "When studying linear algebra, it is common to use elementary row operations to reduce a matrix $A$ to another matrix $B$ such that $B$ contains many zeros. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the entry $a_{11}$ located in the top left-hand corner. We can reduce *all* of the entries *below* it using elementary row operations.\n\nFirst, we subtract $3$ times the first row from the second row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $5$ times the first row to the third row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! We have successfully performed elementary row operations to reduce all of the entries below $a_{11}$ to zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e0cae18-aa8d-5b1d-9aab-4a5334dec0c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4828 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b68078fb-1628-5a76-bb97-1c1261f6641d" :step/math-academy-id 30052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161965 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161757 :question/id #uuid "70991204-370a-5804-a04e-7e664db052de" :question/math-academy-id "e-8048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404530}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8918fe56-d9e1-5eae-97d1-dfc810e443e6" :step/math-academy-id 30053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161966 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161758 :question/id #uuid "cbf8e85c-ffa6-55b9-a087-2a5272f10f25" :question/math-academy-id "e-8049"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526480} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404537}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "cdb6468b-12bc-5624-9941-9d18a55ef6f9" :step/math-academy-id 30054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161759 :question/id #uuid "399b7598-c793-5032-9f33-ac02c7b85fe1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8050"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526485} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404517}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "04f4431c-776f-545b-904c-b0fd1f0a5fab" :step/math-academy-id 30059 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161760 :question/id #uuid "f9912357-1d1d-5413-917e-257dc5dfc0fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8051"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527289} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412026}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "007cc686-090e-50a0-8170-5b1c5eb412f6" :step/math-academy-id 30061 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162827}}] :activity/title "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162182}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412027} :activity/id #uuid "61c7230c-4be1-54c6-95c3-76fe59986f88" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527 :topic/id #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" :topic/math-academy-id 3029} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0166ede7-ac8b-5aee-a3fc-ae0a17866fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326949} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccc7768-32ec-5947-9670-1ec97e53d1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327328} {:answer/id #uuid "54c6de8d-19a8-57aa-96b2-7a92b017ceaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-4{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327667} {:answer/id #uuid "57b8eae7-5346-50fa-98a2-5cd486bfdba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-4{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327701} {:answer/id #uuid "ddddd82a-3c70-51bf-a2eb-edddccc2e4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-4{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326949} :answer-field/id #uuid "326f90b0-553c-55c5-999d-072fa4528fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b4f6183-748c-523a-a6b7-35464ca87075" :question/math-academy-id "q-42918" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 21 & 17 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -18 & -13 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- ${R}_{1}:=…$\n- ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}$\n- ${R}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below ${a}_{34}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 21 & 17 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 21 & 17 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 21 & 17 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -18 & -13 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-3{R}_{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3be10ce9-b323-5320-8a6b-fd2980284abe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39feaea9-f53d-5ee3-a622-cbfc2b43387f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412015} {:answer/id #uuid "3b22a6a4-61fa-5183-9649-c580b3bc7020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412016} {:answer/id #uuid "a66beb93-cf63-56e5-af86-8bf4482be7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412019} {:answer/id #uuid "c49a7ab3-b457-50a7-8db3-96d09691584e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412022} {:answer/id #uuid "caaba1b9-461d-5d04-a1fd-8f442d709eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412015} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7b0ab2-ace0-5e5f-813e-a09b5ee8870d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e471ec15-a8b3-57bf-a5ff-3531b5523c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-91171" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -14 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- ${R}_{2}:=…$\n- ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$\n- ${R}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries below ${a}_{11}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -14 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "481012f0-867f-5ff8-ba1d-2515c1153226" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166feab7-a264-55aa-acc5-4218b97966a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412014} {:answer/id #uuid "4c552dee-9870-5f80-99c4-13a42caad182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+3{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+2{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412017} {:answer/id #uuid "99b872c0-2012-5d57-9afd-16e6b6bde53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412018} {:answer/id #uuid "a66ce929-c767-5507-b64f-0b3826324583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412020} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1f46ac-844a-5bb2-b9b6-04cb4156fcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412021} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff26ed5-7a58-584f-95b3-0a0e85d61774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8820685-174a-5aac-b0a4-a6b54f80941e" :question/math-academy-id "q-91188" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the right of ${a}_{31}=3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9cc0e464-95dc-5890-bc99-303c34184e49"}] :activity/title "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412013}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125299} :activity/id #uuid "8cf4b4e2-a2a4-5056-88ef-3cbe1bd9eadd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050555 :topic/id #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" :topic/math-academy-id 1938} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125736 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a unit vector $\\mathbf{u} = \\left\\langle  u_1, u_2 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nThe **directional derivative of $f$** at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}$ is\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0}\\dfrac{f(\\mathbf x_0 + t \\mathbf u) - f(\\mathbf x_0)}{t}.\n$$\n\nThe directional derivative of $f$ is the rate of change of the function $f$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$ We'll discuss this idea more later in the lesson.\n\nIt turns out that if $f$ is differentiable, we can write $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ in terms of partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u.\n$$\n\nAt the end of the lesson, we'll derive this formula. But for now, let's get some practice using it.\n\nFor example, let's find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)=(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(2\\cdot 2)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+4j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,2) & =∇\\,f(1,2)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(2\\,i+4\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "925cfc95-04b9-5e98-bec4-99d0e4ae3a54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2503 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b456d3e-c653-5713-8112-18d37fb77751" :step/math-academy-id 11245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548196} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548195}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "e45885a4-1bb2-5e1b-b77d-8445bc691679" :step/math-academy-id 11246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125737 :tutorial/content "From the formula for the directional derivative, we get that if  $z = f (x, y)$ is a differentiable function and $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector, then\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'_{\\mathbf u} &= \\nabla f \\cdot \\mathbf u \\[3pt]\n                      &= |\\nabla f| |\\mathbf u| \\cos\\theta\\[3pt]\n                      & = |\\nabla f| \\cos\\theta,\n}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the vectors $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/50/50c82908d5fd619c1c3c45f2aa3ff26929b8f369233c08d0688a091d2d3c123c.png)\n\n \n\nIt follows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\theta =0.$ In other words, $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\mathbf u$ and $\\nabla f$ point in the same direction. Thus, the maximum value of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is $|\\nabla f|.$\n\nA similar argument shows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ reaches its minimum value $- |\\nabla f|$ when $\\theta = \\pi;$ that is, when $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u$ point in opposite directions.\n\nAs a result, we get the following theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f$ increases most rapidly in the direction of the gradient and decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction to that of its gradient.*\n\nFor functions of more than two variables, the same theorem holds." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e4c2cac-e80c-51dd-8138-681fa98ae521" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2505 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as the Direction of Maximum Increase of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "59fc7193-5cff-5a04-9436-423d956e8140" :step/math-academy-id 11253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523235} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523226}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "5e11bfb6-5859-570a-a461-b440db1620c4" :step/math-academy-id 11257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"} :step/id #uuid "67953a72-0311-5e24-ad89-dce2e8e4793b" :step/math-academy-id 11260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125738 :tutorial/content "For a surface $z = f(x,y),$ the partial derivative $f_x(x_0,y_0)$ gives us the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{i} = \\langle 1,0\\rangle.$ But this is just a special case of the directional derivative, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{i}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{j}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the directional derivative generalizes the notion of a partial derivative. \n\nNow, recall that the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ represent the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j,$ respectively. As for a general directional derivative, we obtain the following property:\n\n*The directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0)$ represents the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "40fa091e-c99f-5d19-8681-e9656e23dc2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2508 :tutorial/title "The Directional Derivative as a Generalization of the Partial Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "d49395bc-14f6-5290-8963-de30d5d2fe22" :step/math-academy-id 11285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125739 :tutorial/content "Consider a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ and suppose that the point $P$ with coordinates $(x_0,y_0, f(x_0,y_0))$ lies on $S.$ We wish to derive a formula for the directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0),$ where $\\mathbf u = \\langle u_1, u_2 \\rangle$ is a unit vector.\n\nGeometrically, $f'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0)$ can be constructed in the following way:\n\n- Define a vertical plane $\\Pi$ that's parallel to $\\mathbf u$ and passes through $P.$\n\n- Define the curve $C$ as the intersection of the surface $S$ and the plane $\\Pi.$\n\n- Then, the directional derivative $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$\n\nThe geometric interpretation of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is similar to that of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y.$ The only difference here is that we are not restricting the plane $\\Pi$ to be parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nNow, since $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is tangent to $C$ and is in the direction of $\\mathbf u$ at $P,$ the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle\n$$\n\nis tangent to our surface $S$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/59/59cb8fa410b9194c298d8f477ab0d0cff3e27765bc45c14cb9b5ea9210288184.png)\n\nRecall that $\\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle$ is known to be a normal vector to the surface at $P.$ Since this vector must be perpendicular to $\\mathbf v,$ the dot product of the two vectors must be zero, which means that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle &=0 \\[5pt]\nu_1 f_x (x_0,y_0) + u_2 f_y (x_0,y_0) - f'_{\\mathbf u} &=0 \\[5pt]\nf'_{\\mathbf u} = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u. }\n$$\n\nWe can interpret this formula as the projection of $\\nabla f (x_0,y_0)$ onto the direction of $\\mathbf u,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/82/82e7dece2597e15172600ff598090241facb834a7248ed54286fa777c25a6ebd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "baa62c26-a25a-5293-a28f-128d45e61d68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2509 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula For the Directional Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "61d92228-7cc0-5d7f-93a0-5595777edad8" :step/math-academy-id 11286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125825 :tutorial/content "What is the directional derivative of the function $\\mathbf{f}$ at $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of the gradient? \n\nLet's apply our formula. First, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\nabla\\! f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}){‖}^{2}}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude the following:\n\n*The directional derivative in the direction of the gradient equals the norm of the gradient. Furthermore, the norm of the gradient can be interpreted as the rate of change of the function in the direction of the gradient.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "f8d8fbab-9897-5441-9c15-b0bc1455ed67" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4469 :tutorial/title "The Rate of Change in the Direction of the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "c95e7b32-1764-5c4a-a384-792d274c2f3a" :step/math-academy-id 27522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124730}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501275} :activity/id #uuid "0a41176c-b39d-554a-935e-72997f04c98e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050555 :topic/id #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" :topic/math-academy-id 1938} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ad0a-171c-5322-8cf2-cfca08972fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500510} {:answer/id #uuid "407711da-42ac-56dc-a3f6-af974631b602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500594} {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} {:answer/id #uuid "86e21ca8-366b-5985-a073-18a5cd00683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500749} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c7ab4-1278-5730-804a-3c97ca86d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcae1503-ee5b-5bcc-859b-64edce5a1f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86542" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a60e94a-7f5d-54b3-9400-bd9ba416bdb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8837b541-d3d8-5ebe-8ea8-af61591cb6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322232} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f03f12-e1ac-5472-a11e-f1f87bcd9621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322572} {:answer/id #uuid "c1717e43-53c5-54f5-8c5f-68df4b9cfd08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef68e3-e12b-57d6-890b-83987a1922bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a40605a-36ce-555d-abab-e5a81e672aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3a0c033d-5079-51f1-a44e-c13c59694293" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7cf532da-0f94-51c8-af70-3482c03bc391" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172f3f98-43e9-586f-8bdf-a9ddf4ae061e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500519} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5dcfde-da03-5e9b-8257-b3a15610fd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i+18\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} {:answer/id #uuid "9305e79c-d3f6-5049-b273-0bc89befd2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500772} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2acabe-a8aa-5bd5-a28c-992a7b2ef314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500782} {:answer/id #uuid "e95111ed-e4a8-5a81-b040-27eaaa405bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+9\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} :answer-field/id #uuid "35227601-10b3-594c-aac4-a002a6f61ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c3c8fd2-c84f-5ffa-82e1-4f53ac4509f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130277" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2(xy+1)^{3/2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,j \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,i+3x\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =3(1)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,i+3(3)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+18\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $6\\,i+18\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "892b58dc-320d-53ce-b8f0-06a4365c54d1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb6012-956b-5c39-8935-74645f89ee82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i+2\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500483} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0516fc-fa33-5a32-bc85-021583caaccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500790} {:answer/id #uuid "a91d4692-51f1-5ef6-96a3-68a85bcad337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500817} {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c25011-b246-57b2-afb4-9949bd083e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i+\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} :answer-field/id #uuid "941d1058-20a9-5c97-a5b7-7c5da913add0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-83562" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "d394d923-4ab3-59e3-94fd-7981e3aa060d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501247}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500458}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504723} :activity/id #uuid "e2086f71-2e1e-548f-97d0-b3f66004887f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050555 :topic/id #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" :topic/math-academy-id 1938} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "1a187383-65b5-5345-b337-dc86407bb551" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f931965-4230-53c9-9c4e-51f593f9d015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321267} {:answer/id #uuid "57f67efd-2b45-5e67-a5e4-4b0fd709ba1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321837} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1a8d46-fbc3-5f92-8b69-6709f7023c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322114} {:answer/id #uuid "cda4641b-3880-5a2f-a60a-2d4b84c1090a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322750} {:answer/id #uuid "d4216551-4828-50f1-870a-15d1ba740ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e6c5b32-a96f-564b-b174-aa4099e65f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a13cba05-5f4a-5d75-8f65-f72eb54e0d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-36300" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4765b3cd-4d39-5e89-aa2a-00187802b850" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff85bb-8ce2-5025-a0a1-83fc98417ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321217} {:answer/id #uuid "636513d9-9ad2-5bbe-b8b3-cd67a4bf8d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321920} {:answer/id #uuid "9f98fa5a-9408-52f3-8b7e-8f25e3ab2ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322407} {:answer/id #uuid "c672059e-d68f-556c-9a70-117d11270e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322686} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3b7500-df79-56f9-843a-ede4ff70a4be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a412c3a-6b92-5f8b-8cf9-ec76f9ca97fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36305" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "99beae54-c55d-54a9-84c9-5b047a305e17" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5cc75b-1aa1-5b30-9362-5f8752961da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263250} {:answer/id #uuid "1d98f894-779c-5822-84b3-85b9a53ad6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263271} {:answer/id #uuid "4efb98a8-8a82-5879-9aee-9a09d6afe439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263687} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb30fc7-6c64-5f56-9344-f70ad5eab684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264636} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a55fec-f5cc-59e3-8bae-75fcaf8ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ef6581-d8af-5b79-a84d-4f8dc281dbef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "129e769a-9e81-5bb8-a357-71dd7d678811" :question/math-academy-id "q-130801" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}},\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{(-1)}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0(1)+0(-\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0270345-59d4-5ead-8ae3-ee6cac39422a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371188b1-b7ec-57b7-b295-164f4505e38f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263494} {:answer/id #uuid "468d5107-6a48-54fc-a274-623f490c7ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263617} {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9a260e-6d34-52c4-a50b-774f0edf8d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263781} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f338bb-07ad-5dd5-a554-45630ad32b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b1bccb-f245-5952-bbe0-74fd47a6963c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "234c72df-7b34-5698-b613-f1acebf8fd8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130276" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "ff89b137-8638-5945-ad2a-c71960c68d36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504714}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504029}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116072} :activity/id #uuid "44ee8e90-06fb-5914-8a24-13b8cd43fdef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819 :topic/id #uuid "68fac9fb-a6fb-5c4b-a510-881f3f9dcbd6" :topic/math-academy-id 1685} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116550 :tutorial/content "The properties of indefinite integrals also apply to definite integrals. For example, to evaluate the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{3} 4 x \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwe can use the constant multiple rule and factor the constant out of the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}4x\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(9-4) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the constant multiple rule holds for any constant $k$ and any definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}kf(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =k{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bdf78ae3-dc9e-5db4-a6a4-ac8883d6ab7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 771 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "42942332-9de2-50a2-84a2-31cc9820d6cd" :step/math-academy-id 1479 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115209 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115032 :question/id #uuid "4036785f-d99b-5c01-81e9-edfb93cc1f0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-413"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eaea1ecf-d6b9-5807-8776-17359e94336b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314866}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} :step/id #uuid "21ed1c8e-2514-5eae-bb70-556cf141e5f0" :step/math-academy-id 1480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116551 :tutorial/content "We can also use the sum rule to compute the definite integral of the sum of two functions. For example, to evaluate the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{1}^{2} (x+x^2) \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwe can split up the integral into a sum of two integrals and evaluate each integral separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{2}+\\frac{8-1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4a0dd6d3-abec-56e8-a3b1-a812dfb45c5a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 773 :tutorial/title "Integrating the Sum of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a617fa95-33e7-5e87-86d9-7d7d9736b369" :step/math-academy-id 1482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116075}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115210 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115039 :question/id #uuid "6ecec0d3-1714-550b-bce2-88ead5abf2f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-414"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f236feb9-761b-544b-b34a-19eb46c28988" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341948}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of the Sum of Two Functions Using the Sum Rule"} :step/id #uuid "c61f0b67-9fc5-5ae1-b73c-67633bb488dc" :step/math-academy-id 1483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116552 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of the sum of two functions multiplied by constants, we can combine the addition rule and the constant multiple rule. For example, to compute the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^1 (3x^2 + 6x)\\,\\textrm d x ,\n$$\n\n we can split up the sum and factor out the constants, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute the two resulting integrals using the usual methods.\n\nIn general, for functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and constants $c$ and $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(cf(x)+kg(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+k{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "244b2afb-58eb-526d-ba0c-7ff36d5b834f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 775 :tutorial/title "Combining the Constant Multiple and Sum Rules"} :step/id #uuid "883460b7-eab8-5c86-9349-144425d70c90" :step/math-academy-id 1485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115211 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115041 :question/id #uuid "d5d4992c-4c68-5d30-b7b7-a5a89e4cc3e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-415"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cff6bd5-aeb6-55ed-a44b-fca322e73dd8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314941}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Polynomial Function"} :step/id #uuid "33986316-e319-566e-9931-5531866da50b" :step/math-academy-id 1486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115047 :question/id #uuid "0347b53a-4f7c-587e-9232-aa9be2065576" :question/math-academy-id "e-416"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c5a147-0100-5f4b-a221-25b8674b2115" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314944}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Sum of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8300db68-e37d-5905-a6e3-75198133f507" :step/math-academy-id 1488}] :activity/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Definite Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115456}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163156} :activity/id #uuid "4373edc4-1d40-5e9a-bfe9-ecc0845c2651" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163365 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ respectively. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(X=x, Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively, then\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y.$\n\nIf the random variables are not independent, they are **dependent**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ab3a2705-ec4d-5e72-b6dd-553e9cbb6884" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7746 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b566ab50-5b2d-5693-aaa2-b74aeb985d53" :step/math-academy-id 44847 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161713 :question/id #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12462"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426030}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Indepen